   250	all four REQUIRED, with `additionalProperties` false. `toolchain` is the closed
   251	`toolchain-requirement-v1` object owned by decision 0007: exactly `id` and
   252	`version`, where `id` MUST equal the driver's registry primary toolchain and
   253	`version` is exactly one of the three closed kinds. It is admitted as the fourth
   254	member for one reason and no other — it can only *filter* within the
   255	manager-trusted toolchain set. It cannot name, add, locate, download, activate,
   256	or switch a candidate, and it cannot reach the registry's tested-release
   257	`compatibility` set. Its shape, grammar, ordering, intersection, and diagnostics
   258	are decision 0007's, are not restated here, and MUST NOT be redefined by a
   259	driver contract.
   260	
   261	`buildCommandV6` is frozen at exactly `type`, `driver`, and `source_dir` and
   262	MUST NOT gain `toolchain` or any other member; a schema-6 or schema-7 command
   263	takes its driver's registry baseline requirement, exactly as decision 0007
   264	states.
   265	
   266	Beyond those four members no driver may add a package-controlled member. In
   267	particular a manifest MUST NOT express a binary, product, crate, module,
   268	target-name, feature, profile, configuration, optimization level, argv,
   269	environment, flag, tag, linker option, toolchain path, toolchain root, download
   270	location, mirror, channel or track, version-manager reference, install or
   271	package-manager command, output name or path, install destination, alias, PATH
   272	edit, signing identity, credential, trust root, hook, plugin, macro, generator,
   273	recipe, post-build action, fallback, or secondary artifact. A version constraint
   274	inside the closed `toolchain` object is the single admitted exception to the
   275	toolchain-related half of that list, and it is admitted only in the filtering
   276	sense above.
   277	
   278	Each local driver MUST bind exactly one closed driver-defined project-metadata
   279	file that MUST exist directly in the build root and MUST be the nearest ancestor
   280	of `source_dir`, exactly as `go-v1` binds `go.mod`. A manager MUST NOT discover,
   281	search for, or infer that file, the module, the target, the command, or the
   282	output. Every non-standard module, package, source, embedded, and vendored input
   283	selected by the driver's fixed graph phase MUST remain below the command's build
   284	root.
   285	
   286	Each local driver MUST define a deterministic, non-discovering mapping from
   287	`source_dir` to exactly one compiled program. A driver that cannot define such a
   288	mapping without a new package-controlled member MUST be rejected for this
   289	version. Widening the command object is not an option, because the consuming
   290	manifest command key is the sole executable name and the sole naming authority.
   291	
   292	### 5. External source ownership: `skill-build.json` schema 2
   293	
   294	The external envelope of decision 0005 is unchanged. The consuming manifest owns
   295	`build_repositories` and the command key; the repository owns only the
   296	descriptor; the manager owns acquisition, validation, audit, naming,
   297	publication, and every process.
   298	
   299	The schema-8 external build command, `repositoryBuildCommandV2`, MUST contain
   300	exactly `type`, `driver`, `repository`, `target`, and `toolchain`, all five
   301	REQUIRED, with `additionalProperties` false. `toolchain` is the same closed
   302	`toolchain-requirement-v1` object, admitted under exactly the reasoning of
   303	section 4. `repositoryBuildCommandV1` is frozen at its four members and MUST NOT
   304	gain a fifth.
   305	
   306	`skill-build.json` remains the sole descriptor filename, at the repository root
   307	and nowhere else, with no alias and no implementation-specific name. Schema 2
   308	changes exactly two things and nothing else, in the target object
   309	`skillBuildTargetV2`:
   310	
   311	1. `driver` becomes a `oneOf` over the admitted external identifiers; and
   312	2. one OPTIONAL member is added, `toolchain`, the same closed
   313	   `toolchain-requirement-v1` object.
   314	
   315	`driver`, `build_root`, and `source_dir` stay REQUIRED and keep their schema-1
   316	meaning, `additionalProperties` stays false, and the document still contains
   317	exactly `schema_version` and a non-empty `targets` map. `skillBuildTargetV1` is
   318	frozen at its three members and MUST NOT gain a fourth.
   319	
   320	The descriptor requirement is OPTIONAL rather than REQUIRED because the
   321	repository, not the consuming skill, is the party that knows what its own source
   322	needs, and a repository written before schema 2 must stay readable: an absent
   323	`toolchain` contributes no interval and the effective requirement is decided by
   324	the registry baseline intersected with the consuming manifest's REQUIRED
   325	requirement. Where both are present they intersect, which is associative and
   326	commutative, so no ownership question arises and an empty intersection is a
   327	deterministic host-independent rejection rather than a precedence rule.
   328	
   329	Beyond that OPTIONAL member, a descriptor MUST NOT express a build, install,
   330	test, or run command, argv, environment, feature, profile, configuration,
   331	product, target name, output name or path, toolchain path, toolchain root,
   332	channel or track, download location, mirror, version-manager reference, install
   333	or package-manager command, trust root, platform list, signing identity,
   334	credential, hook, plugin, macro, generator, recipe, post-build action, fallback,
   335	or secondary artifact. A repository declaring a version constraint can only
   336	narrow within the manager-trusted set; it can never introduce a candidate into
   337	it.
   338	
   339	The repository owns the descriptor schema version and the consuming manifest
   340	owns nothing about it. A manager that supports schema 8 MUST read descriptor
   341	schemas 1 and 2. Descriptor schema 1 remains frozen and can express only
   342	`go-repository-v1` targets. A `rust-repository-v1`, `swift-repository-v1`, or
   343	`kotlin-native-repository-v1` command therefore requires a schema-2 descriptor;
   344	against a schema-1 descriptor it MUST fail closed with
   345	`build_descriptor_driver_unsupported`, and against an unsupported descriptor
   346	version with `build_descriptor_schema_unsupported`. Neither failure may fall
   347	back to another target, another driver, `go-v1`, a script, a system command, or
   348	a generic build facility.
   349	
   350	The command and descriptor drivers MUST be equal, and both MUST name the same
   351	external identifier. The whole repository snapshot remains the validation,
   352	identity, and audit subject; only the selected build root is compiler-visible;
   353	and no external repository byte is agent-facing or runtime-copied. The
   354	schema-6 prohibition on a local `build_root` equal to `.` is unaffected by the
   355	descriptor's admission of `.` as a repository root.
   356	
   357	### 6. Toolchain boundary
   358	
   359	The manifest toolchain requirement, trusted resolution, version grammar and
   360	comparison, two-stage preflight ordering, diagnostics, and installation-guidance
   361	catalog are defined by the shared toolchain contract, decision 0007
   362	(`TASK-260728-1g0z69`), and integrated by `TASK-260728-2jaw7h`. This decision
   363	does not restate or redefine them. It supplies the two things that contract
   364	leaves to this one — the version numbers of the schemas that carry it, fixed in
   365	section 1, and the exact wire slots it lands in, fixed in sections 4 and 5 —
   366	and it fixes the boundary properties the version and artifact model depends on:
   367	
   368	1. Every new driver's canonical build input MUST bind a complete trusted
   369	   toolchain identity produced by that contract, as an ordered
   370	   `toolchain_identities` array covering the registry primary toolchain and any
   371	   companions. `curator-go-toolchain-v1` stays frozen and Go-only, `go-v1` and
   372	   `go-repository-v1` keep their existing single-field toolchain identity shape
   373	   byte-for-byte, and no other driver may reuse, extend, or alias either.
   374	2. The trusted toolchain is operator-owned. No manifest, descriptor, repository,
   375	   substitution, or environment value may supply a toolchain path, URL, channel
   376	   or track, mirror, trust root, installer, version-manager reference, or
   377	   package-manager command, and no version of any driver may auto-install a
   378	   toolchain. The closed `toolchain-requirement-v1` object of sections 4 and 5 is
   379	   not an exception to this: it selects nothing, it only intersects an interval
   380	   against the set the operator already trusts, and it cannot reach the
   381	   registry's tested-release `compatibility` set.
   382	3. Every executable started below the worker MUST be a fingerprinted member of
   383	   the driver's declared trusted toolchain closure. When a compiler requires a
   384	   platform linker, SDK, sysroot, runtime library, or archiver that is not inside
   385	   the fingerprinted distribution, the driver MUST bind that component into its
   386	   toolchain identity or MUST reject that platform. A host-resolved tool outside
   387	   the closure is not admissible.
   388	4. Because the toolchain identity is inside the canonical build input, two
   389	   toolchains never alias in the cache, the receipt, the marker, or a claim.
   390	5. Host availability and version preflight runs before source acquisition, and
   391	   the project-metadata compatibility cross-check runs after local validation or
   392	   exact external acquisition and audit and before any compiler work. This
   393	   ordering is owned by the toolchain contract and is restated here only because
   394	   the artifact and audit boundary assumes it.
   395	
   396	### 7. Execution policy and process graph
   397	
   398	Decision 0006 defines `manager-worker-v1` with a Go-bound process graph —
   399	`<GOROOT>/bin/go` and executables below
   400	`<GOROOT>/pkg/tool/<GOHOSTOS>_<GOHOSTARCH>/` — and a Go-bound session of exactly
   401	one `go list` and exactly one `go build`. Its own rejected-alternatives list
   402	records why that is load-bearing: a semantic change to the process graph without
   403	a cache-identity change would let one entry alias another. Reading that identity
   404	generically so it also covers a Rust, Swift, or Kotlin pipeline would reinterpret
   405	a definition that is already bound into frozen rc.4 and rc.5 bytes, and the label
   406	in a published receipt or claim would then no longer mean what the decision that
   407	minted it says.
   408	
   409	This boundary therefore mints a second portable execution-policy identity,
   410	`manager-worker-v2`, and leaves `manager-worker-v1` untouched.
   411	
   412	**`manager-worker-v1` is frozen and Go-only.** Its definition, process graph,
   413	session shape, and wording in decision 0006, `protocol/core.md` section 4.2.1,
   414	and `profiles/manager.md` section 2.2.1 keep their exact meaning and exact
   415	bytes. It stays the policy of `go-v1` and `go-repository-v1` for the whole of
   416	Protocol 1.0. No `go-v1` or `go-repository-v1` canonical build input, logical
   417	cache key, receipt byte sequence, marker record, or claim changes, and the rc.5
   418	candidate is untouched, because nothing that Go binds is re-read.
   419	
   420	**`manager-worker-v2` is a concurrent sibling, not a successor.** The two
   421	identities are both live at Protocol 1.0. v1 is not deprecated, not superseded,
   422	not migrated, and not reinterpreted, and v2 does not replace it. The integer is
   423	an identity discriminator only: a reader MUST NOT infer recency, precedence,
   424	supersession, or containment strength from it, and strength may be compared only
   425	where a specification states the comparison in words. The binding is fixed by
   426	the closed table of section 2, is chosen by neither package data nor operator
   427	configuration, and admits no third combination:
   428	
   429	| Driver | Execution policy |
   430	|---|---|
   431	| `go-v1`, `go-repository-v1` | `manager-worker-v1` |
   432	| the six reserved identifiers | `manager-worker-v2` |
   433	
   434	`manager-worker-v2` carries the identical portable containment contract. The
   435	mandatory portable control set of `protocol/core.md` section 4.2.1, the
   436	exhaustive `rc5-native-control-inventory-v1` inventory, the single
   437	`build_execution_control_unavailable` failure boundary, the exclusion of host
   438	capability evidence from cache identity, and the six deferred hardened
   439	guarantees are the same under both identities, byte for byte and control for
   440	control. No control is added, removed, re-scoped, or weakened. The inventory
   441	keeps its `rc5-` name because its membership is unchanged and inventory
   442	membership never enters a build input, an artifact, or a hashed identity.
   443	
   444	Exactly two things differ, and they are exactly the two things that could not be
   445	said generically about v1 without reinterpreting it.
   446	
   447	First, the two lower graph nodes are bound per driver rather than to Go:
   448	
   449	```text
   450	manager parent
   451	  -> identity-verified manager-owned worker
   452	       -> the driver's fingerprinted trusted launcher
   453	            -> fingerprinted regular executables inside that driver's
   454	               fingerprinted trusted toolchain closure
   455	```
   456	
   457	The launcher and the tool set are the members of that driver's declared trusted
   458	toolchain closure required by section 6 item 3, resolved through decision 0007
   459	and bound into the canonical build input. The upper two nodes — the manager
   460	parent and the identity-verified manager-owned worker — are identical to v1.
   461	
   462	Second, the worker session admits a driver with no graph phase. One v2 session
   463	performs **at most one** read-only graph phase of at most one driver-defined
   464	command, waits while the parent validates the complete graph, accepts exactly
   465	one authenticated build permit, and performs exactly one compile phase of
   466	exactly one driver-defined command. v1 requires exactly one `go list` and
   467	exactly one `go build`; v2's "at most one" graph phase is a different session
   468	shape, which is a second independent reason the identity must differ. The v2
   469	session admits no retry, second graph phase, second compile phase, additional
   470	executable, shell, VCS operation, dependency download, generator, test, run, or
   471	tool request. A driver that cannot map its pipeline onto that session shape MUST
   472	NOT be admitted in this version; widening the session shape further requires yet
   473	another execution-policy identity, a new claim schema version, and its own
   474	review.
   475	
   476	Because both identities are closed constants inside the canonical build input, a
   477	v1 entry and a v2 entry can never alias in the cache, a receipt, a marker, or a
   478	claim, and a reader that knows only v1 cannot mistake a v2 artifact for a
   479	portable Go artifact.
   480	
   481	The per-operation host capability evidence record is re-versioned to
   482	`capability-evidence-v2`, reserved here and minted by the integration task, for
   483	two reasons that are both semantic rather than structural. `capability-evidence-v1`
   484	is normatively closed to `execution_policy: "manager-worker-v1"` — any other
   485	value is `build_execution_hardened_claim_forbidden` — so it cannot report a v2
   486	operation at all. And because section 9 admits a manifest mixing Go and
   487	additional-driver commands in one operation, one operation can be active under
   488	both identities, which v1's exactly-one-record-per-operation cardinality cannot
   489	express. `capability-evidence-v2` keeps the closed member set
   490	`{record_version, execution_policy, platform, controls}` and the probe-once-per-
   491	operation rule unchanged; it admits `execution_policy` drawn from exactly
   492	`{manager-worker-v1, manager-worker-v2}`, and the manager emits one record per
   493	distinct execution-policy identity active in the operation, all sharing that
   494	operation's single probe result and `probed_at`. Any other `execution_policy`
   495	value, including a hardened one, remains
   496	`build_execution_hardened_claim_forbidden`. The record stays result-only and
   497	MUST NOT enter a cache key, receipt input, marker record, or claim, so
   498	re-versioning it moves no frozen byte and `capability-evidence-v1` keeps its
   499	exact meaning for every v1-only operation.
   500	
   501	One consequence of that reservation is stated here rather than discovered later.
   502	The rc.5 corpus and release gate already use `capability-evidence-v2` as their
   503	example of a record version that MUST be rejected: the
   504	`unknown-evidence-record-version-is-rejected` case in
   505	`conformance/v1/vectors/go-host-execution-policy.json` and the "drifted
   506	capability-evidence record" release-gate case both name it. Both are correct at
   507	rc.5, where the version does not exist, and both stay frozen and correct for
   508	rc.5. When rc.6 mints the record, `TASK-260728-251p01` MUST move those two
   509	literals to a version that remains unminted and MUST NOT change the rc.5 corpus
   510	bytes or the rc.5 pin to do so. The boundary gate proves the current state
   511	positively rather than by absence: it requires the frozen corpus to keep
   512	rejecting the reserved version.
   513	
   514	Before the compile phase, each driver MUST apply an exhaustive, deterministic,
   515	pre-compile rejection matrix computed from the validated snapshot and its graph
   516	phase, in the same position where `go-v1` rejects `SysoFiles`, native inputs,
   517	and the non-standard `//go:cgo_import_dynamic` directive. The matrix MUST reject
   518	every package-selected code-execution surface for that language, including build
   519	scripts, procedural and compiler macros, compiler and build-system plugins,
   520	annotation processors, source generators, manifest programs, build tasks and
   520	annotation processors, source generators, manifest programs, build tasks and
   521	recipes, response files, package-selected linkers and native libraries, and
   522	network or registry access. A surface that cannot be rejected deterministically
   523	before the compile phase MUST cause the driver to be rejected, and MUST NOT be
   524	answered by a runtime allowance, an advisory warning, or a sandbox promise. The
   525	shared semantic class for such a rejection is
   526	`build_package_code_execution_forbidden`; each driver contract defines its own
   527	per-surface diagnostics beneath it.
   528	
   529	### 8. Cache, receipt, marker, and claim identity
   530	
   531	`curator-build-source-v1` is unchanged and is reused as the source identity for
   532	every driver in both source modes. It hashes bytes, not language, so no new
   533	source-identity algorithm is created. The protected external snapshot key of
   534	`protocol/core.md` section 9.4 is likewise unchanged.
   535	
   536	Each logical cache key remains the SHA-256 of `CCJ-1` over the complete build
   537	input. Every new build input MUST bind its receipt schema version, driver,
   538	source state for its mode, consuming command name, build root and source
   539	directory selection, native target, complete trusted toolchain identity, and a
   540	closed per-driver policy object whose `execution_policy` is the `const`
   541	`manager-worker-v2`. Inputs from different drivers cannot alias, because
   542	`driver` and the policy object differ, and a v1 input can never alias a v2 input
   543	because the policy constant differs.
   544	
   545	The effective toolchain requirement and the registry `compatibility` set are
   546	gates rather than build inputs, exactly as decision 0007 fixes them, so the
   547	`toolchain` object admitted in sections 4 and 5 never enters a cache key,
   548	receipt, marker, or claim. What enters the build input is the resolved toolchain
   549	identity, not the constraint that filtered it. This is why adding a REQUIRED
   550	fourth member to the schema-8 local command changes no `go-v1` identity: a
   551	schema-8 Go command carries a `toolchain` requirement on the wire and its
   552	canonical build input is byte-identical to the schema-6 and schema-7 input it
   553	already produces.
   554	
   555	Build receipt schema 3 covers the local source mode and schema 4 covers the
   556	external source mode, each as a strict `oneOf` discriminated by the `driver`
   557	`const`, and each carrying that driver's own toolchain identity, native target,
   558	and closed policy object. Receipt schemas 1 and 2 keep their bytes and meanings,
   559	including their `goExecutionPolicyV1` constant `manager-worker-v1`.
   560	
   561	Install marker schema 4 permits `skill_schema_version` through 8 and represents
   562	local-only, external-only, and mixed command sets across receipt schemas 1
   563	through 4 and both execution-policy identities. Every build entry MUST
   564	explicitly record its driver, its `receipt_schema_version`, and its
   565	`execution_policy`, and a reader MUST validate both recorded values against the
   566	two closed tables of section 2 and reject a mismatch rather than infer an absent
   567	value from a driver name. A marker-v4 entry naming a Go driver with
   568	`manager-worker-v2`, or a reserved driver with `manager-worker-v1`, is a
   569	mismatch and MUST be rejected. Marker schemas 1 through 3 keep their shapes.
   570	
   571	Marker v4 generalizes exactly one schema-6 rule. Top-level `build_source` is
   572	REQUIRED exactly when at least one active local build command of any admitted
   573	local driver exists, and MUST otherwise be absent; marker v3's `go-v1`-only
   574	wording does not survive into v4, and marker v3 itself is unchanged. External
   575	entries continue to bind source per entry and MUST NOT use the consuming skill's
   576	raw snapshot as external compiled-source identity.
   577	
   578	Conformance claim schema 4 pins `protocol_version` to the rc.6 candidate and
   579	admits driver assertions for the eight identifiers, each requiring `driver`,
   580	`language`, `execution_policy`, and `operating_systems`. `execution_policy` is
   581	not free within an assertion: it is a `const` selected by the assertion's own
   582	`driver` `const`, `manager-worker-v1` for the two Go drivers and
   583	`manager-worker-v2` for the six others, so the section 2 binding is structural
   584	rather than a prose rule a claim author could violate. The schema admits exactly
   585	those two policy identities and no other, so a hardened claim remains
   586	structurally impossible and needs a later claim version. Claim schemas 1 through
   587	3 keep their bytes and continue to admit only `manager-worker-v1`.
   588	
   589	### 9. Mixed commands, platform claims, credentials, and signing
   590	
   591	Manifest schema 8 MAY mix script commands, system commands, and build commands
   592	of any admitted driver in one manifest and one closure node. Activation,
   593	dependency-command selection, portable-name collision, shim collision, and
   594	provider-first closure rules are unchanged, and active build command names are
   595	still processed in Unicode-scalar lexical order within a closure node. Each
   596	command independently derives its own artifact name, build input, cache key,
   597	receipt, marker entry, and shim, whichever driver it names.
   598	
   599	Mixing therefore reaches both execution-policy identities in one operation. That
   600	is admitted, and it has exactly three consequences, all already fixed above:
   601	each command's own build input binds its own policy constant, so nothing aliases;
   602	`capability-evidence-v2` reports one record per distinct active policy; and the
   603	marker records the policy per entry rather than per marker. The mandatory
   604	portable control set is identical under both identities, so a mixed operation
   605	applies one set of controls, not two.
   606	
   607	This decision makes no platform claim. Each of the six reserved identifiers
   608	starts with an empty qualified-platform set. macOS and Windows remain the
   609	platforms of the portable policy and Linux remains excluded until
   610	`TASK-260728-1skseh`. A driver and platform tuple may enter a claim only when
   611	its driver contract is accepted, its conformance vectors exist, and immutable
   612	native evidence for that exact tuple exists; qualification verifies this. A
   613	platform that cannot satisfy section 3's artifact class or section 6's toolchain
   614	closure is excluded from the claim rather than shipped with a compensating
   615	sidecar, host-resolved tool, or downgraded control.
   616	
   617	Credentials, host-verification state, transport executables, proxy policy,
   618	timeouts, and authentication modes stay operator-owned for every driver and MUST
   619	NOT appear in a manifest, descriptor, repository, compiler environment, receipt
   620	trust field, or marker.
   621	
   622	No driver admitted by this boundary performs manager post-build signing,
   623	timestamping, or notarization, and no package data may select a signing
   624	identity, certificate, entitlement, or notarization credential. A platform
   625	policy that requires a locally signed binary MUST reject the build until the
   626	separately versioned and reviewed signer profile of `protocol/core.md` section
   627	12.2 exists.
   628	
