    90	or leading zeros. Requirements combine as interval intersection, which is
    91	associative, commutative, and order-independent, so the effective requirement
    92	does not depend on the order in which the manager reads its sources.
    93	
    94	**A prerelease toolchain never satisfies any requirement, and a requirement can
    95	never name one.** Prerelease ordering is where cross-language determinism
    96	actually dies: Go writes `1.24rc1` with no separator, Rust uses semver
    97	`-nightly`, Kotlin uses `-Beta1`, and Swift ships dated development snapshots.
    98	There is no shared order over those, and a compiler whose identity is not
    99	stable between two rebuilds is a poor thing to bind into a cache key. Nightly
   100	Rust, Swift development snapshots, and Go release candidates are therefore
   101	rejected as hosts, not ranked.
   102	
   103	### 3. Trusted resolution, never package selection
   104	
   105	Resolution has exactly two admissible origins: a toolchain bundled with the
   106	manager distribution, and trusted operator configuration in manager-owned,
   107	owner-protected state. Everything else is forbidden and named: the ambient or
   108	user `PATH`, any package or repository byte, a runtime root, project
   109	`.agents/bin`, a shim, a manifest or descriptor value, an inherited environment
   110	variable, and any version-manager shim or wrapper. The primary executable MUST
   111	be a regular executable at the entry's fixed relpath inside the tree being
   112	fingerprinted — the generalization of the existing "not a wrapper or an
   113	executable outside the tree" rule for `<GOROOT>/bin/go`.
   114	
   115	The exclusion rule reads on two distinct surfaces, and keeping them apart is
   116	what makes it checkable rather than contradictory.
   117	
   118	On the **manager-defined wire surface** — the manifest build command and the
   119	`skill-build.json` descriptor target — the field set is closed and Curator owns
   120	it. No field naming an executable path, toolchain root, download URL, mirror,
   121	channel or track, version manager, install or package-manager command,
   122	environment override, `PATH` edit, credential, keyring, checksum, or trust root
   123	exists there, and none may be added; any such value is a hard rejection.
   124	
   125	On the **source-ecosystem metadata surface** — files the ecosystem owns, read at
   126	Stage B — Curator does not own the field set, so each entry declares a closed
   127	disposition table giving every field it reads exactly one of `forbidden`,
   128	`compared`, or `ignored`. `forbidden` is reserved for values naming *where* a
   129	toolchain comes from; a channel or track is `compared`, because Curator's
   130	refusal to honor it is exactly what makes it harmless. Forbidden fields are
   131	evaluated before compared ones, in a fixed lexical order, so a file carrying
   132	both has one outcome.
   133	
   134	A version constraint is admitted on the wire surface because it can only
   135	*filter* the manager-trusted set; it can never introduce a candidate into it,
   136	and it cannot reach `compatibility` either.
   137	
   138	Identity generalizes the existing algorithm family. Each entry names
   139	`curator-<toolchain_id>-toolchain-v1`; for `go` that is the deployed
   140	`curator-go-toolchain-v1`, unchanged. A resolved identity is the algorithm
   141	identifier, the normalized native version string, the primary-executable
   142	relpath, and the tree digest. Toolchain location is not portable identity.
   143	
   144	### 4. Two-stage preflight
   145	
   146	Stage A — availability, version, platform — runs immediately after manifest
   147	parsing and build-command validation, for every distinct toolchain in the plan,
   148	in Unicode-scalar lexical order of toolchain identifier, once per operation. It
   149	reads no package byte beyond the already-validated manifest and it MUST
   150	complete before external repository acquisition, before build-cache lookup, and
   151	before any persistent mutation.
   152	
   153	Stage B — source-metadata cross-check — runs per active build command after
   154	local snapshot validation, or after exact external acquisition and audit, and
   155	before the manager reads an artifact-cache candidate or starts a compiler
   156	child. It checks `go.mod`, `Cargo.toml` and `rust-toolchain.toml`,
   157	`swift-tools-version` and `.swift-version`, or the single Kotlin metadata field
   158	that `TASK-260728-168smo` selects.
   159	
   160	Stage B reads declared metadata **as an assertion about the already-resolved
   161	toolchain, never as a selector**. It cannot switch, download, activate, or
   162	re-resolve anything. A `rust-toolchain.toml` `channel` is `compared` and then
   163	discarded — a version literal or `stable` is permitted and never honored, while
   164	`beta`, `nightly`, and dated channels are a mismatch because they assert a
   165	prerelease host v1 never resolves. Its `components`, `targets`, and `profile`
   166	are `ignored`; its `path` is `forbidden`, and being forbidden it is evaluated
   167	first, so a file carrying both `path` and `nightly` is deterministically a
   168	package-influence rejection.
   169	
   170	Neither stage may be skipped by a cache hit, a dry run, or an offline mode, and
   171	neither may be reordered ahead of source validation or audit. Stage A moving
   172	earlier does not weaken any gate: it consumes manager and operator
   173	configuration plus one already-validated manifest field, and every existing
   174	phase keeps its existing position and its existing predecessor set.
   175	
   176	For `go` this costs nothing. Stage A reuses the three package-independent
   177	bootstrap probes that section 4.2 already runs from the manager parent —
   178	`go telemetry off`, `go version`, `go env -json ...` — so the five Go
   179	argument-vector forms remain exactly five and the `manager-worker-v1` process
   180	graph is untouched. Only the phase they sit in changes.
   181	
   182	### 5. Typed diagnostics and manager-owned guidance
   183	
   184	Twelve stable `build_toolchain_*` codes cover requirement validity,
   185	unsatisfiable intersection, availability, undetermined version, incompatible
   186	version, untested release family, prerelease host, untrusted resolution,
   187	unsupported platform, metadata mismatch, package influence, and mid-operation
   188	change. They are listed with their exact trigger and stage in the reference
   189	document. Where two gates would both reject a host, the Stage A step order
   190	decides which code is reported, so the outcome is deterministic.
   191	
   192	A diagnostic carries a `guidance_id`, never prose or a URL. The
   193	`toolchain-guidance-catalog-v1` catalog is manager-owned, versioned, and total
   194	over (toolchain, reason, supported platform) — where `reason` is exactly the
   195	diagnostic code's suffix, so the code-to-reason mapping is the identity and
   196	stays total as codes are added. There is therefore no runtime "guidance
   197	missing" case for any of the twelve, including the authoring and configuration
   198	ones. Each reason declares a `guidance_class` that fixes the admissible origin
   199	of its `primary_source`: the language's own official origin for host reasons,
   200	the manager's operator documentation for configuration reasons, and this
   201	specification for authoring reasons. Every one of those is a manager-trusted
   202	origin, and guidance is text plus URL only — never a package, a mirror, an
   203	installer script, or a command the manager runs.
   204	
   205	Identifiers carry an immutable revision, `toolchain.<id>.<reason>.<platform>.r<N>`,
   206	because a bare tuple cannot name two entries and `superseded_by` would have
   207	nothing to point at. Exactly one entry per tuple is active; supersession moves
   208	to the next revision of the same tuple and retired entries are retained so an
   209	older diagnostic stays resolvable. Because the catalog is presentation, it is
   210	deliberately not a cache, receipt, marker, or claim input: a corrected URL or a
   211	new revision must never invalidate an artifact.
   212	
   213	**v1 never auto-installs.** No download, install, update, activation, or switch
   214	of a toolchain, and specifically not through `rustup`, `swiftly`, `sdkman`,
   215	`asdf`, `mise`, Homebrew, `winget`, the Gradle wrapper, or `GOTOOLCHAIN`.
   216	
   217	### 6. Identity effects
   218	
   219	The resolved toolchain identity stays a build input, as today; new drivers
   220	bind an ordered `toolchain_identities` array covering primary and companions,
   221	while `go-v1` and `go-repository-v1` keep their existing single-field shape
   222	byte-for-byte.
   223	
   224	The effective requirement and the `compatibility` set are **gates, not build
   225	inputs**. Two packages whose different constraints are both satisfied by the
   226	same resolved toolchain produce the same artifact, so putting either in the key
   227	would fragment cache identity while adding no guarantee — the same argument
   228	that kept host capability evidence out of the key in decision 0006. There is no
   229	bypass: Stage A gates before cache lookup, so a hit is only reachable when the
   230	currently resolved toolchain passes both gates.
   360	11.7; `unsupported` continues to mean an unknown driver and MUST NOT be reused
   361	for a toolchain failure.
   362	
   363	## Security impact
   364	
   365	The contract closes the package-to-compiler-selection path for three languages
   366	before their drivers exist, rather than after. Its central claim is narrow and
   367	checkable: a version constraint can only filter a manager-trusted set, so no
   368	manifest, descriptor, or source file can add a candidate, a path, a URL, a
   369	channel, an install command, or a trust root. The `compatibility` set is what
   370	keeps "filter" from degenerating: the trusted set is bounded by manager testing,
   371	not by version ordering, so no constraint spelling can reach a release the
   372	manager has never tested.
   373	
   374	Fingerprinting is honest about what it proves. It proves that the tree is
   375	stable across an operation and identical across operations; it does not prove
   376	upstream authenticity. Verifying that the configured toolchain is genuinely
   377	the vendor's remains the operator's responsibility at configuration time, and
   378	v1 performs no signature verification of a toolchain. Naming this explicitly is
   379	the point: a `content_sha256` in a receipt must not be read as provenance.
   380	
   381	Refusing auto-install keeps the largest untrusted action out of the operation.
   382	Refusing prerelease hosts keeps an unstable compiler identity out of a cache
   383	key that promises reproducibility. Neither refusal claims that a trusted
   384	release toolchain compiling adversarial source is safe; the compiler-input
   385	rejection policy of decision 0004 and the portable containment boundary and six
   386	deferred hardened guarantees of decision 0006 are unchanged and still apply.
   387	
   388	Stage A widens no boundary. It runs earlier on inputs the manager already
   389	trusted at that point — its own configuration and one validated manifest field
   390	— and every gate that preceded compilation still precedes it.
   391	
   392	## Downstream obligations
   393	
   394	- `TASK-260728-2spy93` fixes the next manifest and descriptor schema versions
   395	  and places the `toolchain` object defined here.
   396	- `TASK-260728-2jaw7h` lands the schemas, canonical grammar, ordering, error
   397	  taxonomy, guidance-catalog identifiers, and the positive and negative vector
   398	  inventory listed in the reference document.
   399	- `TASK-260728-12pnm1`, `TASK-260728-1yhuqi`, and `TASK-260728-168smo` MUST
   400	  each complete their reserved registry entry — probe argv, normalization rule,
   401	  prerelease markers, root layout, primary-executable relpath, fingerprint
   402	  algorithm identifier, companion toolchains, supported platforms, baseline
   403	  version, `compatibility` family granularity and initial tested set, and the
   404	  metadata-source disposition table — on a qualified host, and MUST NOT widen
   405	  the exclusion list, reclassify a `forbidden` field as `compared`, admit a
   406	  selector, or introduce auto-install.
   407	- `TASK-260728-ypbuav` owns guidance-catalog maintenance under the lifecycle
   408	  rules: append-only within a version, a new revision of the same tuple for any
   409	  change of meaning, `superseded_by` naming a strictly greater revision of that
   410	  tuple, exactly one active entry per tuple, and totality over all twelve
   411	  reasons enforced by a release gate.

